    "AI judges can hurt systems in two main ways: cost and latency.",
    "Each evaluation by an AI judge is an additional model call.",
    "Using the same powerful model for generation and judging can roughly double API costs.",
    "Evaluating several criteria multiplies calls further.",
    "For example, three evaluation prompts can make the total about four times the generation-only calls.",
    "If the response must be judged before being shown to the user, the judge's inference time is added to the user-facing path.",
    "Added latency may be unacceptable for strict-latency applications.",
    "One way to reduce drawbacks is to use a weaker or cheaper judge when its quality is sufficient.",
    "Using a weaker or cheaper judge reduces both cost and latency.",
    "Another way is to spot-check only a subset of responses rather than judging every response.",
    "Spot-checking a smaller sample costs less and adds less overhead.",
    "Spot-checking may miss some failures.",
    "Choosing the sampling rate based on the required confidence is recommended.",
    "Evaluating more responses increases confidence but also increases cost.",
    "Using judging asynchronously or outside the return path is possible when immediate evaluation is not required.",
    "The context supports the latency concern but does not provide detailed implementation guidance for asynchronous judging.",
    "Cheaper judges and selective evaluation reduce expense and overhead.",
    "Pre-return judging improves risk control at the cost of user-visible latency.",
    "The acceptable trade-off depends on the application's confidence and latency requirements."
] 

